{
 "rule": "For each gating comparison of an arm A with its reference R (T2 s0 ON vs adapter OFF = Titan-Sparse; each extra Titan-Sparse adapter vs OFF; each extra Supa adapter vs Supa 1.7) on a sealed confirmation draw minted after the dev selection of its surface-diversity setting, for each of the two graders g (the primary typed grader and the lenient extraction grader) and each selected category c (75 items): lost = items R answers correctly and A does not; gained = items A answers correctly and R does not; net = gained - lost (an unknown_twin item is correct only if both twins are). p = two-sided exact McNemar p-value (binomial on the lost + gained discordant items, 1/2); p_Holm = Holm-Bonferroni adjustment of p over the selected categories of that comparison and grader. Category c has a DETECTED LOSS under g iff lost > gained and p_Holm < 0.05, and EXCEEDS ITS MARGIN under g iff net < -11 (more than 11 of its 75 items lost net). The pooled net under g is the sum of net over the selected categories; it EXCEEDS ITS MARGIN iff it is below -0.04 x the number of selected items (-24 for 600 items). Comparison verdict: LOSS DETECTED iff some category has a detected loss under either grader; otherwise MARGIN EXCEEDED iff some category or the pooled net exceeds its margin under either grader; otherwise RETAINED. GO (the retention guard passes for arm A) iff RETAINED, reported as GO (k/m binding), where k of the m selected categories are binding, i.e. R answers at least the fewest items whose loss with no gain would fail the category alone (9 when 8 categories are selected); LOSS DETECTED and MARGIN EXCEEDED are NO-GO. Report-only, never gating: the per-category flag net < -5 of 75 (a point estimate, not a non-inferiority test), Titan-Sparse (OFF) vs Supa 1.7 and T2 s0 vs Supa 1.7 (core and cumulative drift, verdicts computed the same way), excluded categories, accuracies with Wilson 95% intervals, EOS-termination rates, abstention components, the operating characteristics, and the AUROC of the per-item first-token KL(R || A) for predicting correctness flips. The suite is templated and narrow: a RETAINED verdict is a floor, not a proof of general retention.",
